const title = "Dishwasher Air Gap: Required in California? LA Guide";
const description = "What a dishwasher air gap is, why California code (CPC §807.3) requires one when most states don't, why a high loop isn't a CA substitute, and how to clear a spitting air gap yourself. Same-day LA dishwasher repair, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is a dishwasher air gap required in California?",
    a: "Yes. The California Plumbing Code (Section 807.3, adopted from the Uniform Plumbing Code) says no domestic dishwasher may be directly connected to the drainage system or a food-waste disposer without an approved dishwasher air-gap fitting on the discharge side. California is one of the states — along with Washington, Minnesota, and Hawaii — that mandate the physical air gap; many other states follow the International Residential Code and allow a 'high loop' instead. So in Los Angeles the air gap isn't optional or old-fashioned — it's the code-required method. The listed air gap must be installed with its flood-level (FL) marking at or above the flood level of the sink or drainboard, whichever is higher."
  },
  {
    q: "What does a dishwasher air gap actually do?",
    a: "It prevents backflow — dirty water from the sink or garbage disposer siphoning back into your dishwasher and contaminating clean dishes. The air gap creates a physical break in the drain line: the dishwasher's drain hose runs up to the air-gap fitting on the countertop, and a second hose carries the water back down to the disposer or drain. Because there's an open gap of air in that path, contaminated sink water can never be pulled back up into the dishwasher, no matter what happens downstream. That's the whole reason the plumbing code cares about it — it's a cross-connection / public-health protection, not just a drainage detail."
  },
  {
    q: "I don't have an air gap on my LA dishwasher — what does that mean?",
    a: "It means your setup likely predates the current code or was installed with a high loop instead, which is common on older LA homes and on dishwashers plumbers installed the 'IRC way.' Practically, an existing high loop usually still drains fine and isn't dangerous day to day. But under the California Plumbing Code an air gap is what's required, so it can come up at a home inspection, a permitted remodel, or a dishwasher replacement — and a new install in LA should get an air gap to be code-compliant. If you're replacing a dishwasher or selling the home, that's the moment to add one. We can install the air gap when we service or swap the unit."
  },
  {
    q: "Water is spitting out of the air gap onto my counter — how do I fix it?",
    a: "That's the classic clogged-air-gap symptom, and it's usually a quick, safe DIY. Water backs up and spits out the top because something downstream of the air gap is blocked — most often the ridged hose between the air gap and the disposer, or debris inside the air-gap cap itself. Pop off the chrome or plastic cap, unscrew the inner cover, and check the fitting for gunk; then check the lower hose for a clog. The single most common cause on a brand-new disposer is that the installer forgot to knock out the dishwasher plug inside the disposer inlet — the water has nowhere to go and backs up to the air gap. If clearing the air gap and hose doesn't fix it, the drain hose or pump is the next suspect and it's time for a tech."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use a high loop instead of an air gap in Los Angeles?",
    a: "Not as a code substitute. A high loop — strapping the dishwasher drain hose up under the countertop before it runs down to the disposer — is the accepted method in International Residential Code states, and it does help prevent backflow. But the California Plumbing Code §807.3 specifically requires an approved air-gap fitting, so a high loop alone is not a compliant replacement for the air gap in California. Plenty of LA homes have a high loop and drain fine, but if you want it done to code — especially on a new install, a permitted job, or before a sale — the air gap is what the code calls for. A high loop is better than nothing, but it isn't the CA-approved answer."
  }
];

